# AI workflow — StageScore

This file executes two accepted ADRs: **0013** (tier triage on top of SDO) and
**0016** (model routing by phase, with a floor that is never lowered). It adds
no policy of its own. If it conflicts with an ADR or an accepted Spec, the
ADR/Spec wins and this file is the defect.

`session-hygiene.mdc` owns *when to cut the chat*; do not restate it here.
Written in English because only agents act on it (ADR 0015).

## Announce before working — one line, in the Orchestrator's language

> "Tier M (`stagescore/lib/pdf`, ~4 file, không vùng nhạy cảm) → Sonnet, build
> theo Spec 0060."

| Tier | When | Gates |
|---|---|---|
| **S** | Fix inside already-accepted behavior, small radius | none — still bound by "never invent product behavior" |
| **M** | One feature, or a complex bug | Spec accepted at **G3**, verify at **G4** |
| **L** | Sensitive area, a change to an accepted ADR, or a change that spans this repo and the web repo | **G2** ADR + **G3** Spec + **G4**, plus Security Review |

- An S task growing past ~5 files, or reaching outside accepted behavior →
  **STOP**, announce the new tier, follow its gates.
- Ambiguity discovered mid-build is a signal to **go back to G3**, not a signal
  to raise the model.
- Unsure between two tiers → ask one question instead of guessing.

## Never-downgrade areas — top-tier model, no justification needed

The StageScore floor, as amended into ADR 0016 § Sàn không được hạ (2026-08-08).
The web repo's list — auth, billing, LiveKit, R2 — describes files that do not
exist here, so these are the paths that carry the same asymmetry:

- **Audio ownership / timing:** `lib/metronome/**`, `lib/sync_map/sync_map_playback.dart`
- **Destructive data paths:** `lib/library/library_backup.dart`, delete paths in
  `lib/library/score_library.dart`, any `**/*_persistence.dart`
- **Anything leaving the device:** `lib/brand/brand.dart` URLs, share extension,
  `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`, `docs/product/STORE-LISTING.md`
- **New platform channel, native audio, or OMR vendor** — also a hard stop for the human
- **Editing an accepted ADR**

The first two are the ones `git revert` cannot undo: a wrong `formatVersion` or a
wrong delete branch is a musician's data on their own device, not a line of code.

## Model by phase (ADR 0016) — the default, before any override

| Phase | Judgement | Tokens | Model |
|---|---|---|---|
| Explore, inventory, search, read-to-understand | low | very high | cheapest, **always via subagent** |
| Decide — ADR, Spec, grilling, interface design | very high | low | top-tier |
| Build against a locked Spec | medium | medium | mid-tier (session default) |
| Review | high, and needs another angle | medium | different family than the author |
| Mechanical — rename, format, translate, boilerplate | low | medium | cheapest |

Escalate only on a real signal: a second failed attempt on the same problem,
tests still red after one deliberate fix, or the agent's own stated uncertainty.
Log the escalation in one line, so a Spec that keeps needing it becomes visible.

## Model by logic type — overrides the phase default per subtask

- **Pattern-following** → cheap model: a menu row from an existing sheet, l10n
  strings, a persistence file mirroring another one, tests copied from a sibling.
- **Design-heavy** → a strong model writes the state machine, the invariants and
  the test list **into the Spec first**; a cheap model only fills in the approved
  design. No approved design → do not delegate. In this repo design-heavy means:
  gesture arena, zoom/pan lifecycle, scroll anchoring, PageTurn timing,
  MeasureMap/SyncMap/metronome clocks, PageOrder.
  *Evidence:* Spec 0033 reopened twice and both root causes were framework
  timing — `kDoubleTapTimeout` disambiguation, then `InteractiveViewer` inertia
  writing to the controller after `onInteractionEnd`. Neither was missing code,
  so neither was findable by a model implementing line by line.
- Always pass `model` explicitly to a subagent. It defaults to `inherit`, so an
  expensive lead silently spawns expensive workers.
- Review a diff with a **different model family** than the one that wrote it.
- Give a worker its slice — Spec section, file list, acceptance check — never the
  run history. A worker expanding scope beyond the Spec is a defect, not a bonus.

## Context budget — where the bill actually is

ADR 0016 measured it: **89% of the cost was the build phase running in one long
chat**, and the deliberately expensive decide phase was 2.4%. So the levers are
about *volume*, not about model names.

- **G3 accept is a chat boundary** (ADR 0016 § Một chat, một pha): the chat that
  locks a Spec does not build it, and one chat carries at most one Spec. A
  "continue" on an already-long chat is a signal to cut the chat, not an
  instruction to keep going — measured once at 42% of a whole chat's cost.
- Exploration, inventory and "read to understand" always go to a **cheap
  subagent** — its tokens then never enter this chat.
- **Never full-read a file over ~800 lines.** Grep first, then Read with
  `offset`/`limit`. The two most-edited files are also the two most expensive to
  hold: `lib/ui/pdf_mode_screen.dart` (~2.8k lines), `lib/ui/library_screen.dart`
  (~2.3k).
- **Never read or edit `lib/l10n/gen/**`** — 22k generated lines. Edit
  `lib/l10n/app_*.arb` and regenerate.
- **Never full-read** `docs/product/DECISIONS-LOG.md` (~250 KB ≈ 60k tokens) or
  `docs/adr/0019-*.md` (~81 KB). Read the tail, or grep the one heading needed.
- The metric to watch is **agent turns in one chat**, not the model name. Past
  roughly 100 turns, a single chat is the largest line item of the day.

## Verify commands

**An agent may run** (seconds), from `stagescore/`:

```bash
flutter analyze
flutter test -r failures-only test/<one>_test.dart   # while iterating
flutter test -r failures-only                        # once before handover
```

Always pass `-r failures-only`. The default reporter prints one line per test,
so a green full suite (653 tests, ~40s wall) otherwise dumps 653 lines into the
chat and every later turn pays for them again.

Flutter needs to write its own cache, so these commands **fail inside the
sandbox** ("Operation not permitted" on `bin/cache/engine.stamp.tmp`). Request
full permissions on the first try rather than burning a round trip on the error.

**Only the Orchestrator runs:** `flutter build apk|appbundle|ipa`, Gradle,
Xcode, and every on-device demo. An agent polling a multi-minute build costs
more than the single error line it returns — ask for the failing tail instead.

This repo has **no CI**, so the only automatic gate is the versioned pre-push
hook `.githooks/pre-push`, which runs `flutter analyze` then
`flutter test -r failures-only` and refuses the push if either fails. It is what
ADR 0016 § *Gate tự động* assumes exists when it calls a cheap model safe. A
fresh clone does not have it — `.git/hooks` is not versioned — so install it
once with:

```bash
ln -sf ../../.githooks/pre-push .git/hooks/pre-push
```

The hook is a backstop, not a substitute: it still runs only at push time, and
nothing at all catches a skipped G3 except the Orchestrator.

## Reporting, and the remediation circuit breaker

Separate **Evidence** (a command that was run, plus its output) from **Not
verified** (and why). The shape to copy is Spec 0033's own note: *"301 tests
xanh, analyze sạch. Chưa demo lại trên thiết bị thật."* A successful compile is
never runtime evidence, and gesture, timing and layout behavior stays unverified
until the Orchestrator demos it on a device.

After **two** remediation rounds on the same finding, **STOP**. Do not start a
third with the same model: report what keeps breaking and ask whether to
escalate the model, write a design note, or cut scope.

## Spec and log hygiene — a cost rule, not a tidiness rule

- **One Spec, one open question.** A `done` Spec that reopens gets a *new* Spec.
  Appending "Cập nhật sau G4" essays means every later session re-reads the whole
  history at full price — 0033 and 0052 (63 KB) are the warnings.
- A Spec heading past ~200 lines, or covering three unrelated behaviors at G3,
  should be **split**. 0054 bundled three and cost a 193-turn build; cutting the
  Spec at G3 was the cheapest lever available.
- `DECISIONS-LOG.md` rows are one or two sentences. Anything longer belongs in
  the Spec or in an ADR — the log is read far more often than it is written.
- A bug fix carries a regression test. Commit messages explain the root cause
  (why), not the diff (what).
- Only commit when the Orchestrator asks.
